# Line endings belong to the repository, not to whoever checked it out.
#
# Two of this project's gates compare the text of a tracked file against text a
# program produced. `node polygon/verify.mjs --check-readme` compares the
# verification table in `polygon/README.md` with the table the run just rendered,
# and the guards in `tests/docs/` read tracked documents the same way. A program
# joins its lines with "\n" and with nothing else. Without this file a
# contributor on Windows — or anyone whose editor writes CRLF — gets a working
# tree full of "\r\n", and every one of those comparisons then fails on a
# difference nobody made. Worse than the failure is what it says: the table is
# out of date. The fix it suggests, regenerating the table, commits the wrong
# line endings and cures nothing. Found by the audit of 14 August 2026 (K-4).
#
# `eol=lf`, not `text=auto` on its own: `text=auto` normalises what goes *into*
# the repository and leaves the checkout to `core.autocrlf`, which Git for
# Windows turns on when it is installed. The working tree is what the gates read,
# so the working tree is what has to be settled here.
#
# The comparisons themselves no longer depend on this — they normalise line
# endings before comparing, which is where the actual fix for K-4 lives. This
# file is the other half: a repository whose contents mean the same thing on
# every machine, so that the next comparison written does not have to remember.
#
# Every tracked file is text today and there is nothing to mark `binary`. One
# added later needs a line of its own, or git will mangle it on the way in.
* text=auto eol=lf
